#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056005 :tutorial/content "A **compound statement** is a sentence that consists of two or more statements separated by logical connectors, such as the word \"and\" or the word \"or.\"\n\nFor example, the following sentence is a compound statement:\n\n$P: 2$ is an integer **and** $2$ is positive\n\nThe first statement is \"$2$ is an integer,\" and the second is \"$2$ is positive.\" These two statements are separated by the logical connector \"and.\"\n\nThe following sentence is also a compound statement:\n\n$7$ is odd **or** $7$ is even\n\nIn this case, the first statement is \"$7$ is odd,\" and the second statement is \"$7$ is even.\" These two statements are separated by the logical connector \"or.\"\n\nWe can express the logical connectives \"and\" and \"or\" using symbols:\n\n- The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\land$\". Notice that it resembles \"A\" for \"And.\"\n- The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor$\".\n\nLet's translate the following sentence into symbolic form:\n\n$2$ is an integer **and** $2$ is positive\n\nTo start, we introduce the letters $A$ and $B$ to denote each individual statement:\n\n- $A:$ $2$ is an integer\n- $B:$ $2$ is positive\n\nThen, our sentence becomes\n\n$$\\underbrace{2 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is an integer} }_A \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{2 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is positive} }_B,$$\n\nthat is, $A \\land B.$\n\nFinally, note the following terminology:\n\n- The statement \"$P$ and $Q$\" is called the **conjunction** of the statements $P$ and $Q.$\n- The statement \"$P$ or $Q$\" is called the **disjunction** of the statements $P$ and $Q.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4570766f-a355-5a80-9756-cd94d76fed9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7962 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056029 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the truth values of the logical connector \"and\". We have the following key idea:\n\n*The statement $A \\land B$ is true if $A$ is true **and** $B$ is true. Otherwise, it is false.*\n\nLet's determine the truth values of the following compound statement:\n\n$2$ is positive **and** $2$ is even.\n\nWriting this in symbols, we have the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nor, simply\n\n$$A\\land B.$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}A∧B≡\\text{true}∧\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIn this context, the symbol \"$\\equiv$\" means \"is logically equivalent to.\"\n\nLet's now consider a similar compound statement but with the $2$ replaced with a $3$ in the second statement.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$A\\land C$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $C$ is false, this compound statement is false:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}A∧C≡\\text{true}∧\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e16d77df-210e-5934-90ce-44b096916261" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8142 :tutorial/title "Truth Values of Statements Containing the \"And\" Connective"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056072 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the truth values of the logical connector \"or\". We have the following key idea:\n\n*The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true. Otherwise, it is false.*\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, consider the following statement: $$\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}$$ Since $A$ is true and $B$ is true, this compound statement is true: $$\\begin{aligned}A∨B≡\\text{true}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}$$\n- Next, consider the following statement: $$\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}$$ Since $A$ is true, this compound statement is true: $$\\begin{aligned}A∨C≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}$$\n- Now, consider the following: $$\\begin{aligned}{{-2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{D}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}$$ Since $B$ is true, this compound statement is true: $$\\begin{aligned}D∨B≡\\text{false}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}$$\n- Finally, consider the following: $$\\begin{aligned}{{-2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{D}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}$$ Since $D$ and $C$ are both false, this compound statement is false: $$\\begin{aligned}D∨C≡\\text{false}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b0d24cd-f605-59b2-9f16-f053e9b74e8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9044 :tutorial/title "Truth Values of Statements Containing the \"Or\" Connective"}]}
